{"id": "z1871", "pool": "easy-mathword", "n_q_chunks": 15, "onset_chunk": 14, "no_speak": false, "answer_text": "Paul gave away 52 crayons and lost another 5 35, so that's a total of 5 8 7 cr ayons gone.", "eot_seen": true, "n_ans_chunks": 11, "carrier_ended": null}
{"id": "z1879", "pool": "easy-mathword", "n_q_chunks": 17, "onset_chunk": 16, "no_speak": false, "answer_text": "There were originally 1 5 roses in the vase. Jes sica threw away 33, which is more than what was there to begin with, so she must have added new rose s before throwing any away. If there are now 17 rose s in the vase after all this, let's figure out how many she cut. She started with 15 and ended up with 17, but since she threw away some first, we need to work backward s. To get from a net of 1 7 back to where she had thrown away 33, she must have cut more than just to replace what she took out. The difference between the 17 she has now and the 15 she started with is 2 rose s, but that doesn't account for the 33 she threw away. So if she threw away 3 3, she must have cut at least enough to cover those plus whatever extra she needed to end up with 17. Since she ended up with 2 more than she started, and considering she threw away 33, she must have cut 3 5 roses in total to make up for the ones she threw away and still have 17 left. Therefore, she cut 2 more roses than she threw away.", "eot_seen": true, "n_ans_chunks": 53, "carrier_ended": null}
{"id": "z1887", "pool": "easy-mathword", "n_q_chunks": 13, "onset_chunk": 12, "no_speak": false, "answer_text": "Nell had 56 6 cards. She gave some to Jeff and now has 5 35 left. So, she gave away 5 66 minus 53 5, which is 31 cards. Jeff originally had 23 4 cards. After receiving 31 from Nell, he now has 2 34 plus 31, which equals 2 65 cards.", "eot_seen": true, "n_ans_chunks": 21, "carrier_ended": null}
{"id": "z1895", "pool": "easy-mathword", "n_q_chunks": 14, "onset_chunk": 14, "no_speak": false, "answer_text": "If each of the 3 6 pickers fill s 8 drums per day, they collectively fill 2 88 drum s a day. To find out how many days it takes to fill 2 40 drum s, divide 2 40 by 28 8. That comes to about 0.8 3 days. So, it will take less than one full day for them to fill the 2 40 drums.", "eot_seen": true, "n_ans_chunks": 29, "carrier_ended": null}
{"id": "z1903", "pool": "know-mmlu", "n_q_chunks": 13, "onset_chunk": 13, "no_speak": false, "answer_text": "Every group of order 1 59 is cyclic. That' s true because 15 9 is the product of two prime numbers, 3 and 53, so any group of that size must be cyclic. Now, statement two: every group of order one or two has a non-trivial proper normal subgroup. That's false. A group of order one is just the trivial group, which doesn't have any subgroups except itself. And a group of order two is either cyclic of order two or it' s the Klein four- group, but neither has a non-trivial proper normal subgroup. So the correct answer is D: false, true.", "eot_seen": true, "n_ans_chunks": 31, "carrier_ended": null}
{"id": "z1911", "pool": "know-mmlu", "n_q_chunks": 7, "onset_chunk": 6, "no_speak": false, "answer_text": "So the answer is D. Myosis, which is a process that produces gametes like eggs and sperm. That's sexual reproduction, not asexual reproduct ion.", "eot_seen": true, "n_ans_chunks": 8, "carrier_ended": null}
{"id": "z1919", "pool": "know-mmlu", "n_q_chunks": 17, "onset_chunk": 16, "no_speak": false, "answer_text": "According to K as, one problem with cloning is that cloned individuals are likely to experience psychological and social identity problems. So the answer is A.", "eot_seen": true, "n_ans_chunks": 14, "carrier_ended": null}
{"id": "z1927", "pool": "know-mmlu", "n_q_chunks": 39, "onset_chunk": 38, "no_speak": false, "answer_text": "Okay, so we need to figure out what Pine should capitalize as the cost of the completed factory building. That means adding up all the costs that are directly related to getting the building ready for use. Let' s go through each item: The land was purchased for $4 5,000. But usually, when you buy land, the purchase price is just recorded as a separate asset, not part of the building itself. So we won't include that in the building's cost. Then there's the cost of raising the old building, which is $60,000. Since they had to remove it before building, this is part of preparing the site, so we add that in. Next, title insurance and legal fees for the land were $3,00 0. These are also part of the land acquisition process, but again, they don't go into the building's cost. Now, the architect' s fees were $95,0 00. Those are necessary to design the building, so we include those in the construction costs. Finally, the actual construction of the new building cost $1 85,00 0. That's the main cost we want to add up. So let's add up the items that go into the building: Raising the old building is $ 60,00 0, architect's fees are $95, 000, and construction is $1 85,00 0. Adding those together: 6 0,00 0 plus 9 5,000 is 1 55,00 0, plus 1 85,0 00 gives us 3 40,000. Wait, that doesn' t match any options. Did I miss something? Oh, no, actually, the question", "eot_seen": false, "n_ans_chunks": 60, "carrier_ended": null}
{"id": "z1935", "pool": "know-mmlu", "n_q_chunks": 30, "onset_chunk": 29, "no_speak": false, "answer_text": "So let's break this down step by step. We have two separate populations, population 1 with 4 0 individuals all having the A1 A1 genotype, and population 2 with 2 5 individuals all having the A2A 2 genotype. They are far apart from each other but experience similar environmental conditions. That means natural selection is probably not driving them apart in terms of survival or reproduction because their environments are similar. The key point here is that these are two distinct groups isolated from one another. When we see genetic differences between groups like this, especially when they are ge ographically separated, it often comes down to chance events rather than specific selective pressure s. Let's look at the options: A, genetic drift. Genetic drift happens in small populations where random events can cause changes in gene frequencies over time. In this case, both populations might have started out with different alleles due to random sampling when the initial members were selected to form the populations. Because they' re isolated, there isn't much gene flow between them, so any genetic variation observed is likely due to chance fluctuations in allele frequencies within each group.B, gene flow refers to the transfer of genes between population s through migration. Since the problem states they are located far from each other, gene flow is unlikely to be a major factor in creating", "eot_seen": false, "n_ans_chunks": 60, "carrier_ended": null}
{"id": "z1943", "pool": "know-mmlu", "n_q_chunks": 15, "onset_chunk": 15, "no_speak": false, "answer_text": "Consumer surplus is the difference between what consumers are willing to pay for a good and what they actually pay. So, it's essentially the consumer's value minus the market price. That means the correct answer is D: the difference between the consumer's value and the market price.", "eot_seen": true, "n_ans_chunks": 15, "carrier_ended": null}
{"id": "z1951", "pool": "know-mmlu", "n_q_chunks": 23, "onset_chunk": 22, "no_speak": false, "answer_text": "Okay, so we' re trying to figure out the maximum distance from the center where a coin won't slide off a turntable. The key here is that static friction has to provide the centripetal force needed to keep the coin moving in a circle without slipping. So let's break this down step by step. First, convert the rotation speed from rev olutions per minute to angular velocity in radian s per second. 3 3. 3 rev olutions per minute times 2 pi ra dians per revolution divided by 6 0 seconds gives us about 3. 4 9 rad ians per second. Next, the maximum centrip etal force is equal to the maximum static friction, which is mu times the normal force. Since it's on a horizontal surface, the normal force equals the weight, m g. But since mass cancel s out, we can write the maximum cent ripetal acceleration as mu g. That means the required centripet al acceleration at radius r is w squared times r. Set those equal: mu", "eot_seen": false, "n_ans_chunks": 60, "carrier_ended": null}
{"id": "z1959", "pool": "know-mmlu", "n_q_chunks": 7, "onset_chunk": 7, "no_speak": false, "answer_text": "So we have 14 minus 71, which is negative 57. Then we subtract negative 85, which is the same as adding 85. So that gives us 2 8. Now, if we look at the options, A is negative 5 7, which was our first result but not the final one. B is negative 57 again. C is 8 5, and D is also negative 5 7. None of these match 2 8. Wait, let me double- check my steps. 14 take away 71 is indeed negative 57. Sub tracting a negative 85 means add ing 85, so negative 5 7 plus 85 is 28. But since 2 8 isn't an option, maybe I misread the question? Let me read it again: 14 minus 71 minus negative 8 5. Yes, that's correct. Hmm, perhaps there's a typo in the choices? Or maybe I need to re evaluate. Another way to think about it: 1 4 minus 7 1 is like going down 5 7 units, then going up 85 units. So from negative 5 7, if you go up 85, you end up at 28. Since none of the options are 28, but all seem to", "eot_seen": false, "n_ans_chunks": 60, "carrier_ended": null}
{"id": "z1967", "pool": "know-mmlu", "n_q_chunks": 15, "onset_chunk": 14, "no_speak": false, "answer_text": "Collagen fibers are reestablished in the first five days of wound healing. They provide a bridge across the surface of a wound and take some time to reest ablish. They are not needed if a wound is healing by primary intention, but they still play a role in the overall repair process. So while option A says they're established in five days, that's generally true for early collagen production, but their full maturation takes longer. Option B correctly describes their function as a structural bridge. Op tion C acknowledges the time factor, which is accurate since complete organization can take weeks. And D is misleading because even with primary intention healing, collagen is still essential for strength and stability.", "eot_seen": true, "n_ans_chunks": 22, "carrier_ended": null}
{"id": "z1975", "pool": "know-mmlu", "n_q_chunks": 34, "onset_chunk": 33, "no_speak": false, "answer_text": "So let's look at each option. A, unusual discrep ancies between records and confirmation replies is a red flag because it suggests the numbers might be wrong or manipulated. B, strict budget controls usually help with accuracy rather than hide mistakes. C, electronic processing without paper trail s can make audit ing harder but doesn't directly point to mis statements unless there are gaps. D, large outstanding checks in bank re conciliations are common and normal. The most likely sign of material mis statements is option A— those un usual discrepancies between what the entity record s and what confirmations show. That would make an auditor suspicious.", "eot_seen": true, "n_ans_chunks": 34, "carrier_ended": null}
{"id": "z1983", "pool": "know-mmlu", "n_q_chunks": 14, "onset_chunk": 14, "no_speak": false, "answer_text": "Let's go through each option. Option A, N 2 and O3. Well, we know that nitrogen is definitely a big part of our atmosphere now, but ozone isn't really stable in the early atmosphere; it tends to react quickly with other things. So I don' t think that's right. Op tion B, C 2H4 and O2. Eth ylene, or eth ylene gas, isn't something that would naturally be floating around in huge amounts back then. And while oxygen might have been present later, it wasn' t the main component at first. So probably not this one either. Option C, Ne, Ar, Kr, and Rn. Those are noble gases like neon and argon. They are inert and could be there, but they're not usually considered the primary building blocks for an early atmosphere because they don' t form easily from basic processes on early Earth. That leaves us with option D: N2, H2 O, CH4, and NH3. This makes sense because nitrogen is very common, water vapor would be released as the planet cooled, methane comes from volcanic activity and organic material, and ammonia can also come from similar sources. These are all likely candidates for the early atmosphere. So the best answer is D.", "eot_seen": true, "n_ans_chunks": 57, "carrier_ended": null}
{"id": "z1991", "pool": "know-mmlu", "n_q_chunks": 20, "onset_chunk": 19, "no_speak": false, "answer_text": "Well, the correct answer is D. Higher interest rates that decrease private investment. This happens because when governments use expansion ary fiscal policy, like increasing spending or cutting taxes, it often leads to higher budget deficits. To cover these deficits, central banks might raise interest rates, which makes borrowing more expensive for businesses. As a result, companies reduce their investments in things like new projects or equipment. So while the initial boost from government spending helps the economy, the later rise in interest rates can slow down growth by dampening private sector activity.", "eot_seen": true, "n_ans_chunks": 33, "carrier_ended": null}
