{"id": "z2175", "pool": "know-mmlu", "n_q_chunks": 13, "onset_chunk": 12, "no_speak": false, "answer_text": "The correct answer is C, scapego ating. This practice involves identifying a specific group as the source of all societal issues, which diverts attention from broader systemic problems and often leads to unfair treatment or prejudice against that group.", "eot_seen": true, "n_ans_chunks": 15, "carrier_ended": null}
{"id": "z2183", "pool": "know-mmlu", "n_q_chunks": 28, "onset_chunk": 27, "no_speak": false, "answer_text": "So the question is, what happens to the equilibrium price and quantity of good Z when the price of a close substitute X rise s? When the price of a substitute goes up, consumers tend to switch from the more expensive substitute to the relatively cheaper good Z. This means demand for Z increases. An increase in demand shifts the demand curve for Z to the right. As a result, both the equilibrium price and quantity of Z will rise. Therefore, the correct answer is C: the equilibrium price and quantity will both rise.", "eot_seen": true, "n_ans_chunks": 31, "carrier_ended": null}
